  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An active weather pattern will continue into the\nweekend as an upper trough digs into the eastern CONUS and a\nweakening cold front approaches from the northwest. \n\nThe threat for severe storms increases today as the upper \ntrough axis pushes across the area bringing increasing jet \ndynamics with the attendant weakening cold front pushing into \nthe region. Instability continues to be forecast to be a bit \nhigher tomorrow with MLCAPES forecast to peak around 2000-2500 \nJ/Kg with similar shear in place. Pwat values will continue to \nbe north of 2\" bringing the threat for wet microbursts producing\ndamaging wind gusts and SPC has parts in the Mid-Atlantic \noutlooked in marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for severe storms. \nGreater upper level support and convergence along the sfc front \nand other lingering boundaries may also lead to locally heavy \nrainfall bringing an increased risk for flash flooding and WPC \nhas a slight risk (level 2 of 4) for excessive rainfall across \nthe region. \n\nContinued unsettled on Saturday but the upper trough begins to\nlift north of the area with height rises aloft and weakening  \ndynamics throughout the day bringing a decreasing threat for\nsevere thunderstorms but there remains a marginal risk (1 of 4)\nfor excessive rainfall. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Upper ridging builds into the region from the \nwest for the early to mid portion of next week bringing lower \nchances for showers and thunderstorms, though near climo chances\nfor storms along the sea breeze will persist through peak \nheating each day. The main story will be the building heat with \nhighs expected to climb into the 90s early next week and peaking\nin the mid 90s as we move into the middle of the week with heat\nheadlines possible WED and THU with MaxAppT in the range of\n105-115deg possible.",
